data.content:
Here are the idiomatic MSTest replacements:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0);
    Assert.IsTrue(users.Any());              // no built-in "IsNotEmpty"

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive));
    CollectionAssert.AllAreSatisfied(users, u => Assert.IsTrue(u.IsActive));
    // or keep: Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive));

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.Count() == 3);
    Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count);         // expected first, actual second

    // Assert.IsTrue(admin != null);
    Assert.IsNotNull(users.Single(u => u.Role == "Admin"));
}
```

**Key improvements:**

| Instead of | Use | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count > 0)` | `Assert.IsTrue(x.Any())` | MSTest lacks `IsNotEmpty`; `Any()` is clearest |
| `Assert.IsTrue(count == 3)` | `Assert.AreEqual(3, count)` | Shows *expected vs actual* on failure |
| `Assert.IsTrue(obj != null)` | `Assert.IsNotNull(obj)` | Failure message says "was null" instead of "was false" |
| LINQ `.All()` check | `CollectionAssert.AllAreSatisfied` | Available in MSTest v3+; gives per-element failure info |

The main win is **better failure messages** — `Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count)` reports `Expected: 3, Actual: 5` instead of just `Assert.IsTrue failed`.
